## Remainder and Assign

The `%=` operator computes the remainder and assigns it back:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var x = 17;
  x %= 5;  // x = 17 % 5 → x = 2
  return x;
}
```

### Complete Compound Assignment Table

| Operator | Name | Example (x=10) | Result |
|----------|------|----------------|--------|
| `+=` | Add-assign | `x += 5` | `15` |
| `-=` | Subtract-assign | `x -= 3` | `7` |
| `*=` | Multiply-assign | `x *= 4` | `40` |
| `/=` | Divide-assign | `x /= 2` | `5` |
| `%=` | Remainder-assign | `x %= 3` | `1` |

### Practical Uses of %=

```xiom
// Keep a value in range 0-59 (like seconds on a clock)
var seconds = 55;
seconds += 10;    // 65
seconds %= 60;    // 5 — wrapped around!

// Cycle through 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2...
var index = 0;
for i in 0..10 {
  index += 1;
  index %= 3;  // Keeps index in 0..2
}

// Check even/odd pattern
var state = 1;
state += 1;    // 2
state %= 2;    // 0 (even)
state += 1;    // 1
state %= 2;    // 1 (odd)
```

### All Together Now

Here are all five compound operators in one example:

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var value = 100;
  value += 50;   // 150
  value -= 30;   // 120
  value *= 2;    // 240
  value /= 7;    // 34
  value %= 10;   // 4
  return value;
}
```

